## 1. Introduction
Hydrogen \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\) energy has been regarded the most promising energy carrier and an alternative to fossil fuel energy because of
its high energy density and eco-friendly characteristics [1–3]. Compared to the current industrial \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production technologies, water electrolysis has been identified as a more ecofriendly and sustainable method [4–6]. However, the slow kinetics of the anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) severely restrict the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate and energy efficiency [7–10]. In this case, other anodic reactions with low thermodynamic potentials are expected to replace the OER, among which the UOR is a suitable substitute because of its low thermodynamic potential of \(0.37\,\mathrm{~V~}\) versus reversible hydrogen electrode (vs. RHE) and the free source of urea wastewater [11–13]. Nonetheless, owing to the slow \(6\mathrm{e^{-}}\) transfer process of the UOR with the equation: \(\mathrm{CO(NH_{2})_{2}(a q)+6O H^{-}\rightarrow N_{2(g)}+5H_{2}O_{(1)}+}\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2(g)}+\mathrm{Se^{-}}\) , the UOR usually requires a high performance electrocatalyst to boost efficiency [14]. Thus far, noble metal catalysts such as Rh and Pt have achieved high catalytic activity for the UOR. Unfortunately, their scarcity and high cost prevent them from being commercialized [15–17].
Nickel-based catalysts, such as \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) [18,19], \(\mathrm{NiCoO_{4}}\) [20], and spinel \(\mathrm{Ni}_{1.5}\mathrm{Mn}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [21] have been well developed and exhibit impressive UOR performance [22,23]. However, these studies are just in their infancy, and there is much room for improvement in their UOR performance. Increasing the active site density by structural engineering and enhancing the intrinsic activity by surface/ interface engineering are two important and effective strategies to further improve the catalytic performance of nickel-based catalysts [24–28]. Currently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with tunable pore structures, homogeneous active metal node distributions, modifiable chemical compositions, and highly designable flexibility have been extensively studied as platforms/precursors for developing high-performance electrocatalysts [29,30]. However, conventional postprocessing of MOFs, such as thermal pyrolysis, usually requires high temperatures and not only uses considerable energy but also leads to the deactivation of active sites due to structural sintering [31,32]. In view of the instability of most MOFs, the electrochemical strategy would be an efficient and mild alternative to thermal pyrolysis for achieving in situ surface modification/reconstruction [33]. Furthermore, the electrochemical activation of bulk MOFs can destroy nonconductive organic ligands in MOFs but keep the metal frameworks intact, resulting in hierarchical porous stable catalysts with more exposed catalytically active sites [34,35]. In addition, regulating the electronic structure of the catalytically active centers by heteroatom doping has been proven to effectively improve the intrinsic activity of catalysts [36–39].
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All the reagents were analytically pure and used without any purification treatment. \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was purchased from Guangdong Guanghua Sci-Tech Co., Ltd. \(\mathrm{C}_{9}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}\) was purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd., \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((50\mathrm{\wt}\%)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((25\ \mathrm{\wt}\%)\) were obtained from Chengdu Cologne Chemicals Co., Ltd. The \(\mathrm{Pt}/C\) electrocatalyst was from Suzhou Sinero Technology Co., Ltd, and nickel foam (NF) was from Lyrun New Material Co., Ltd.
## 2.2. Synthesis of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs
NF was cut into \(0.5\,\times\,1.0\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) pieces first, and then, NF was cleaned with \(5\,\mathrm{\vt.\%}\) HCl, ethanol, and water in turn. Typically, \(0.035\,\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , \(0.07\,\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{C}_{9}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}\) , and \(28\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.\) - \(\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were completely dissolved in a mixture consisting of \(8\,\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol and \(8~\mathrm{mL}\) of ultrapure water and stirred continuously for \(10\;\mathrm{\min}\) ; the mixture was then transferred to a \(20\,\mathrm{\mL}\) Teflonlined stainless autoclave. The cleaned NF was added to the abovementioned solution, along with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((25\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) . After \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) of hydrothermal reaction at \(160\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , a NiMn-BTC precursor was obtained and cleaned with ethanol and ultrapure water several times and then dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) . Finally, the precursor was electrochemically activated using scanning CV in a threeelectrode system. The CV test was performed in \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) KOH with a scanning rate of \(10\,\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}\) and with a potential window of 1.269–1.469 V (vs. RHE). After a continuous CV cycle (400 cycles), the target catalyst \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs were acquired. For comparison, contrasting samples, \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) powders and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs, were also synthesized. The preparation processes for \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) powders and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs were similar to those of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs, except that NF and \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) were not added during the synthesis process, respectively.
## 2.3. Preparation of Pt/C catalysts
The drop casting method was used to prepare the \(\mathrm{Pt}/C\) catalyst. \(5~\mathrm{mg}\) of commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/C\) \((20\,\mathrm{wt}\%)\) powder was mixed with \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of Nafion solution \((5~\mathrm{wt}\%)\) , \(50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ultrapure water, and \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ethanol for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) with sonication to obtain a well-mixed solution. The \(20~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}\) solution was dropped on the cleaned NF and allowed to dry. The loading quality of \(\mathrm{Pt}/C\) on NF was \(\sim2~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}\) .
## 2.4. Characterization
FEI Quanta 400F was used for scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and an FEI Tecnai G2 F20 instrument was used for transmission electron microscopy (TEM). X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis was performed in the range from \(5^{\circ}\) to \(85^{\circ}\) with a scanning speed of \(10^{\circ}\,\mathrm{\min}^{-1}\) . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed on an EscaLab \(\mathrm{Xi}+\) system equipped with an Al-Kα X-ray source \(\mathrm{hv}=1486.6\,\mathrm{eV})\) . The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface area of the samples was determined with nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms acquired at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) with an ASAP 2460 V3.01 surface area and porosity analyzer.
used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. The target catalysts were used as the working electrode with a working area of \(0.5\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), which was exposed to the electrolyte. Electrochemical in situ activation via the CV method proceeded in \(1.0\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH with a scanning rate of \(10\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). LSV was carried out in \(1.0\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH with or without \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\) urea at a scanning rate of \(5.0\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). All the potentials were transformed with respect to RHE with the equation: \(\mathrm{E_{RHE}=E_{SCE}+0.0591\;pH+0.242}\). Furthermore, the transformed potentials were also corrected by IR compensation with \(80\%\). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed at frequencies ranging from \(0.01\) to \(10^{6}\;\mathrm{Hz}\). The Tafel slopes were calculated with the equation \(\eta=\mathsf{b}\;\log\left|\mathsf{J}\right|+\mathsf{a}\), where \(\mathsf{b}\) is the Tafel slope \((\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\), \(\mathsf{a}\) represents the current density \((\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\), and \(\eta\) is the potential (V).
The electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) of the catalysts was obtained by calculating the double-layer capacitance \((C_{\mathrm{dl}})\) by measuring the CV curves at various scan rates (20, 40, 60, 80, and \(100\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\)) in a non-Faradaic region (1.02–1.12 V vs. RHE). Subsequently, a linear trend can be observed by plotting the average current density at \(1.07\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE \((\Delta J=(J_{\mathrm{anodic}}-J_{\mathrm{cathodic}})/2)\) with respect to the scanning rates. \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) was represented by this linear slope, which was proportional to the ECSA of the electrocatalyst.
## 3. Results and discussion
The \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs were successfully synthesized via the in situ electrochemical conversion of bulk NiMn-BTC MOF. The synthetic process of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs is illustrated in Fig. 1 (see details in the Experimental section). The color of NF changes from metallic white to green and then to black in Fig. S1, indicating that the surface composition of NF changes during the conversion process. Fig. 2a shows the XRD pattern of NiMn-BTC, indicating that peaks between \(5^{\circ}\) and \(40^{\circ}\) are identical to peaks of the precursor \(\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((\mathbf{M}\ =\ Z\mathbf{n}\), Ni, and Co) [40,41], and the peaks at \(\sim44^{\circ}\), \(52^{\circ}\), and \(76^{\circ}\) are from the NF substrate. After in situ electrochemical conversion, the original diffraction peaks between \(5^{\circ}\) and \(40^{\circ}\) disappeared, the newly formed phase is consistent with \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) (JCPDS No. 01–1047), and the obtained \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) can be further confirmed by Raman spectroscopy, as shown in Fig. S2 [42,43]. Furthermore, typical SEM and TEM measurements revealed the morphology of the as-obtained materials. Fig. S3a–c displays the SEM images of NiMn-BTC; the nanowire arrays grew uniformly on NF. The smooth surface of the NiMn-BTC nanowire is visible in the TEM image (Fig. S3d), and the overall needle-like nanowire array morphology is preserved (Fig. 2b–d). Notably, the enlarged image of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs (Fig. 2d) shows that the surface of nanowires became rather rough, which can increase the specific
surface area, thereby facilitating the exposure of active sites for the UOR. Furthermore, the morphology of \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs without Mn doping was also studied and is illustrated in Fig. S4; the constructed \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs possess a microstructure similar to that of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs. Fig. S5 exhibits the morphology of Mn-Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) powders, which possess messy nanowires with different sizes, indicating that the NF plays an important role as a carrier during the \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNA synthesis process. The TEM image of the \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNA nanowires (Fig. 3e) further confirms the rough surface of the \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNA nanowire. The polycrystalline structure of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNA is demonstrated by a SAED pattern with two diffraction rings. The HR-TEM image of Mn-Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs (Fig. 2f) shows that the interplanar distance of Mn-Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) is \(2.33\;\mathrm{\AA}\), which is attributed to the (101) plane of Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\). The elemental mapping analysis performed on the Mn-Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanowire using STEM-EDS reveals the uniform distribution of Ni, O, and Mn (Fig. 2g–k). The EDS analysis shows that the atomic percentage of doped Mn is \(\sim7.1\) at.%.
The nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs exhibit type-IV isotherms (Fig. 3a), indicating the existence of mesopores in \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs [44,45]. The corresponding pore size distribution curves (inset in Fig. 3a) further verify the mesoporous architecture, and the average pore size is approximately \(7.48\;\mathrm{nm}\). The BET surface area of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs is \(7.89\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which is slightly larger than that of NiMn-BTC \((6.09\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), Fig. S6). The enhanced BET surface area can be attributed to the increase in surface roughness of the of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNA. The porosity of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs with a mesoporous nature and larger specific surface area are beneficial for the accessibility of electrolytes and provide more exposed electroactive sites for the UOR. The XRD patterns of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs and \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs are shown in Fig. S7, expect for the peaks of \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\), no observable Mn-related diffraction peaks are detected, demonstrating that Mn is doped into \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) instead of participating in the reaction to form compounds [46]. The surface composition and surface electronic states of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs were also investigated by XPS. Figs. S8a and S8b depict the valence states of two different compounds before and after electrochemical conversion using the high-resolution spectra of Ni 2p and Mn 2p from NiMn-BTC and \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs. During electrochemical conversion, a part of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) is converted to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) due to the oxidation reaction [47], which is derived from the redox reaction observed during CV scanning (Fig. S9). The high-resolution Ni 2p XPS spectrum of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs (Fig. 3b) shows that the binding energy peaks at \(\sim854.7\) and \(872.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) are attributed to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and are the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\), and the binding energy peaks at 856.1 and \(874.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) are attributed to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) [48–50]. In particular, the Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\)
peaks of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs are slightly shifted toward higher binding energies than those of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs, revealing electronic interaction occurred after doping Mn into \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\). The Mn 2p XPS peak (Fig. 3c) can also be detected, with two main peaks seen at \(642.1~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(653.8~\mathrm{eV}\), indicating that the Mn species doped in \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) is \(\mathtt{M n}^{4+}\) [51,48].
The UOR catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs was primarily evaluated in \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) KOH with \(0.5~\mathrm{M}\) urea using a three-electrode system. Fig. 4a compares the performances of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs for the OER and UOR. The potential for the UOR of \(254\;\mathrm{mV}\) is much less than that for the OER, attaining a current density of \(100\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) and illustrating the outstanding UOR performance
of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs. In addition, the UOR catalytic performances of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs, \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs, NiMn-BTC, \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) and bare NF are exhibited in Fig. 4b. The \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs show the best UOR activity with the smallest potential of \(1.37~\mathrm{V}\) to afford a current density of \(50~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\). The much lower potential of Mn-Ni \(\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}\) PNAs than that of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs (1.41 V) indicates the positive effect of Mn doping on the UOR activity improvement. Meanwhile, the LSV curves of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs and \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) powders in Fig. S10a show the obviously advantageous structure of PNAs over that of the powders. Meanwhile, the UOR catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs is also better than that of previously reported Ni-based catalysts (Fig. 4c and Table. S1).
To further determine the catalytic kinetics of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs for the UOR, the corresponding Tafel plots were obtained. As shown in Fig. 4d and Fig. S10b, the \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs have a lower Tafel slope of \(31~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}\) than NiMn-BTC \((48\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) , \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs \((45\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) , \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) ( \(43\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) ), bare NF \((46\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) , and \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) powders \((67\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) , indicating the favorable catalytic kinetics of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs. Furthermore, EIS was used to determine the charge transfer capability of the as-synthesized catalysts. The \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs exhibited a lower charge-transfer resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)\) than the NiMn-BTC, \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs, and Mn-Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) powders (Fig. 5a and S10d), suggesting the faster charge transfer and electrocatalytic kinetics in \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs. In addition, the smallest \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) also demonstrates the significance of in situ electrochemical conversion for charge transfer capability enhancement and three-dimensional nanowire arrays for transport kinetics improvement. The \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) value of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs \((7.6\;\mathrm{mF}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) is higher than the \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) values of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs \((2.9\;\mathrm{mF}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) , NiMnBTC \((6.1\ \mathrm{~mF~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) , and \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) powders \((1.3\ \mathrm{~mF~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) (Fig. 5b, c, S10c, and S11), indicating that \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs possess a larger ECSA than \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs, NiMn-BTC, and \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) powders.
Evaluation of the durability of catalysts is also significant in terms of their practical applicability. The durability of Mn-Ni \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs in an alkaline urea solution was measured via chronopotentiometry. The potential of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni(OH)}_{2}\) PNAs only increases by \(0.03\%\) after a \(48\;\mathrm{h}\) test at \(1.0\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , which is much
smaller than the increase in potential of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs \((1.51\%)\) and that of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) powders \((5.11\%)\), showing the remarkable long-term stability of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs for the UOR in alkaline media. Moreover, the long-term stability at a higher current density of \(100\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) also shows the substantial durability of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs (Fig. S15), demonstrating their potential in industrial hydrogen production. Furthermore, the structure and composition of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs after the UOR durability test were further checked. SEM images show that the morphology of the \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs is unchanged (Fig. S16). The XRD and XPS results show that the chemical composition and elemental valence of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs remain consistent with those before, except for the increase in \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) (Fig. S17), owing to more \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) transforming to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) during the UOR durability test.
Due to the excellent activity of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs toward the UOR, the anodic electrocatalyst can combine with another outstanding cathodic catalyst to electrolyze water and urea to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). Therefore, an anode of \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs and a cathode of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) are used to constitute a two-electrode electrolysis system. The HER performance of the \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) electrode shows nearly no difference with and without urea, indicating that urea has little effect on \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) activity for the HER in alkaline media (Fig. S18). Fig. 6a shows that the \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}|\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs needed a small cell voltage of \(1.40\ \mathrm{V}\) at a current density of \(20\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) during overall urea electrolysis, which is much lower than that needed by \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}|\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) (1.64 V). Meanwhile, a cell voltage of \(1.50\ \mathrm{V}\) is required to obtain a higher current density of \(50\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) for urea electrolysis, while a higher cell voltage of \(1.65\ \mathrm{V}\) is required to afford the same current density for water electrolysis (Fig. 6b). The greatly reduced cell voltage indicates that the thermodynamically favorable UOR can be used to replace the slow OER to significantly improve the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation efficiency. The long-term durability test of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}|\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNAs was performed for overall urea splitting at a current density of \(20\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\). Fig. 6c shows that there is only a \(1.40\%\) potential increase after \(48\ \mathrm{h}\) of urea electrolysis, indicating that the \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}|\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNA couple has excellent durability for overall urea electrolysis. Therefore, the \(\mathrm{Mn-Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) PNA catalyst can be used as an efficient and inexpensive electrode for urea electrolysis systems. In addition, regarding the low cell voltage, a commercial \(1.50\ \mathrm{V}\) battery was used as the power source to successfully drive urea-assisted \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation with many visible air bubbles at the anode and cathode (Fig. 6c), showing its portability in daily life.
